---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoToMp3Batch from '@/components/tools/media/VideoToMp3Batch.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="webm-to-mp3">
  <VideoToMp3Batch
    client:load
    from="webm"
    hint="WebM video, .weba audio and browser voice notes, decoded on your device"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>WebM to MP3: Opus in, MP3 out</h2>
    <p>
      Almost every WebM file stores its audio as Opus, a codec that is better than MP3 at every bitrate, most
      of all below 96 kbps, where MP3 sounds watery and Opus still sounds like speech. Conversion from Opus to
      MP3 therefore loses efficiency. Do it when you need MP3: for a car stereo, an old hardware player,
      transcription software, a DAW that does not import WebM, or a client who asked for MP3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because of that loss, use a generous bitrate. 192 kbps or higher keeps the second compression inaudible
      on music; for speech, 128 kbps mono is more than enough and halves the file.
    </p>

    <h2>Where WebM audio comes from</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Source</th>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Typical audio</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Browser voice recorders and web apps</td>
            <td>.webm or .weba</td>
            <td>Opus, mono, 48 kHz</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Browser and tab screen recordings</td>
            <td>.webm</td>
            <td>Opus, stereo</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Saved web video</td>
            <td>.webm</td>
            <td>Opus at 128-160 kbps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Meeting and call recorders</td>
            <td>.webm</td>
            <td>Opus, often mono at a low bitrate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Telegram voice messages</td>
            <td>.ogg or .webm</td>
            <td>Opus, mono</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>.weba files also work</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>.weba</code> file is a WebM container with only an audio stream. Browsers use this extension when
      a recording had no camera. The tool handles it the same way, and because there is no video track to
      remove, it converts almost at once.
    </p>

    <h2>Voice notes and transcription</h2>
    <p>
      This is the most common use. A web app recorded an interview, a stand-up or a lecture, and the
      transcription service, the note app or the recipient needs MP3. For this job:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Choose <strong>128 kbps</strong> and tick <strong>downmix to mono</strong>. Speech recognition gains nothing from stereo or a higher bitrate.</li>
      <li>An hour of audio is about 55 MB, which fits in an email.</li>
      <li>The conversion runs in this tab, which matters when the recording is a client call, a medical appointment, an HR meeting or a legal interview.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Batch conversion</h2>
    <p>
      Add as many recordings as you like. They convert one after another, and you can download them as one
      ZIP. There is no file count limit.
    </p>

    <h2>To keep the video</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/webm-to-mp4">WebM to MP4</a> converts the whole file, picture included, for an editor or an
      iPhone. If a browser screen recording cannot be scrubbed, that page explains why and fixes it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
